640-15.</Citation><ArticleIdList><ArticleId IdType="doi">10.1128/AEM.03640-15</ArticleId><ArticleId IdType="pmc">PMC4959502</ArticleId><ArticleId IdType="pubmed">26873322</ArticleId></ArticleIdList></Reference><Reference><Citation>Tveit AT, Hestnes AG, Robinson SL, Schintlmeister A, Dedysh SN, Jehmlich N, et al. Widespread soil bacterium that oxidizes atmospheric methane. Proc Natl Acad Sci USA. 2019;116:8515&#x2013;24. doi: 10.1073/pnas.1817812116.</Citation><ArticleIdList><ArticleId IdType="doi">10.1073/pnas.1817812116</ArticleId><ArticleId IdType="pmc">PMC6486757</ArticleId><ArticleId IdType="pubmed">30962365</ArticleId></ArticleIdList></Reference><Reference><Citation>Bodelier PLE, P&#xe9;rez G, Veraart AJ, Krause SMB (2019). Methanotroph Ecology, Environmental Distribution and Functioning. In: Lee EY editor. Methanotrophs: Microbiology Fundamentals and Biotechnological Applications. Cham: Springer International Publishing; 2019. p. 1&#x2013;38.</Citation></Reference><Reference><Citation>Thompson LR, Sanders JG, McDonald D, Amir A, Ladau J, Locey KJ, et al. A communal catalogue reveals Earth&#x2019;s multiscale microbial diversity. Nature. 2017;551:457&#x2013;63. doi: 10.1038/nature24621.</Citation><ArticleIdList><ArticleId IdType="doi">10.1038/nature24621</ArticleId><ArticleId IdType="pmc">PMC6192678</ArticleId><ArticleId IdType="pubmed">29088705</ArticleId></ArticleIdList></Reference><Reference><Citation>Wartiainen I, Hestnes AG, McDonald IR, Svenning MM. Methylobacter tundripaludum sp. nov., a methane-oxidizing bacterium from Arctic wetland soil on the Svalbard islands, Norway (78 degrees N) Int J Syst Evol Microbiol. 2006;56:109&#x2013;13. doi: 10.1099/ijs.0.63728-0.</Citation><ArticleIdList><ArticleId IdType="doi">10.1099/ijs.0.63728-0</ArticleId><ArticleId IdType="pubmed">16403874</ArticleId></ArticleIdList></Reference><Reference><Citation>Reddy KR, Rai RK, Green SJ, Chetri JK. Effect of temperature on methane oxidation and community composition in landfill